A note on scope, for readers rather than for lawyers.

The Commons Clause above removes one right from the MIT grant: you may not
sell the Software, or sell a product or service whose value comes
substantially from it. Everything else MIT allows, you may still do — read it,
run it, change it, and pass it on, including changed versions, as long as it
is free.

This makes Superb's code source-available rather than open source, and the
project says so plainly wherever it describes itself.

